        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Thapsus</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">6 April 46 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near Thapsus, beside the Marsh of Moknine, in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, with twelve legions (five newly raised and seven veteran), a large number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and slingers and 3,000 to 5,000 cavalry, around 60,000 to 70,000 soldiers; the Optimates under Quintus Caecilius Metellus Scipio, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>, eight Roman and three Numidian legions, 14,000 to 16,000 cavalry, about 20,000 light infantry and 60 elephants, around 90,000 soldiers in all</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s army won. The Optimate army broke and its camp was stormed. Scipio, Cato, Juba and Petreius died by their own hands soon afterwards, though the civil war went on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
