    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> fought with a smaller army than his opponents: about 60,000 to 70,000 soldiers against around 90,000, who also had 60 elephants.</li><li>The elephants decided much of the fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Archers</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s right drove those opposite them back into their own men, and the veterans of Legio V held the charge on his left.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s soldiers killed around ten thousand of the enemy after the battle, in spite of his repeated orders to spare them. The battle is generally regarded as the last large-scale use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> in the West.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After the battle Legio V Alaudae is said to have been awarded an elephant as its symbol, because five of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> had sustained the charge of the elephants on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s left.</p></aside>
    </section>

