    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">49 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> crosses the Rubicon with the 13th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> and the last civil war of the Republic begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s foraging force is nearly overcome by Petreius and Labienus near Ruspina but withdraws to the town</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> besieges Thapsus and Scipio marches round the Marsh of Moknine to meet him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s army defeats the Optimate army at Thapsus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cato kills himself at Utica, and Juba and Petreius die in a duel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 46 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-thapsus" data-v="a44c294427" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up facing each other</b> Scipio set his legions in three lines in the centre, with cavalry and light infantry on the flanks and elephants in front of them. Caesar also used three lines, with archers and cavalry on the flanks and half of the fifth legion in reserve.</li><li><b>Caesar's lines advance</b> A trumpeter of the seventh legion sounded the attack. Caesar saw his right surge forward and ordered a general advance.</li><li><b>The archers panic the elephants on one flank</b> Caesar's archers on the right flank attacked the elephants facing them. The animals panicked, turned and trampled their own men.</li><li><b>The elephants on the other flank charge</b> The elephants on the other flank charged against Caesar's left. The five cohorts of the fifth legion, placed behind that flank, met the charge.</li><li><b>The fifth legion turns the elephants back</b> The legionaries stabbed their pila at the elephants' eyes and weak spots and sounded their trumpets. The beasts turned and ran towards their own lines.</li><li><b>Scipio's left breaks, then the rest</b> After the elephants were lost, Scipio began to lose ground. His left broke first and the rest of his army followed, with Caesar's lines pressing after it.</li><li><b>The siege legions overrun Afranius' camp</b> The two legions left at the siege marched south to the troops fortified opposite Afranius and Juba's camp. Together they attacked and overran Afranius' camp.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="Julius Caesar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Julio_C%C3%A9sar_(26724093101)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ángel M. Felicísimo from Mérida, España, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last civil war of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> began in 49 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> defied senatorial orders to disband his army after the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He crossed the Rubicon with the 13th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>, in violation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Optimates fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. Caesar followed and was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>, but recovered and beat Pompey at Pharsalus. Pompey fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was assassinated.</p><p>The remaining Optimates, not ready to give up, regrouped in the African provinces. Their leaders were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-younger">Cato the Younger</a> and Quintus Caecilius Metellus Scipio. Other figures from the nobility in the resistance were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>, Publius Attius Varus, Lucius Afranius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-petreius">Marcus Petreius</a> and Pompey's sons Sextus and Gnaeus. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a> was a valuable local ally. After the pacification of the Eastern provinces and a short visit to Rome, Caesar followed his opponents to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>He had gathered six legions around Lilybaeum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, with four more on their way from Rome. Despite poor weather he sailed and reached the African coast on 28 December, landing near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Hadrumetum</a>. A storm had scattered his transports, leaving him with just 3,000 infantry and 150 cavalry. Hadrumetum was held by a strong garrison, and Caesar found he had neither the men nor the materiel to take it. Warned that a large force of enemy cavalry was coming, he marched south, harassed by Numidian light cavalry, and reached Ruspina on 29 December.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> had twelve legions at Thapsus: five newly raised ones (XXV, XXVI, XXVIII, XXIX and XXX) and seven veteran ones (V, VII, VIII, IX, X, XIII and XIV). The veteran legions had campaigned for many years and were all understrength. He also had a large number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and slingers and 3,000 to 5,000 cavalry, so that he had around 60,000 to 70,000 soldiers when he arrived at Thapsus.</p><p>The Optimates had eight Roman and three Numidian legions, around 55,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a>, with 14,000 to 16,000 cavalry, about 20,000 light infantry and 60 elephants. Their army totalled around 90,000 soldiers. Earlier in the campaign Juba's arrival had brought their force up to thirteen legions, and Scipio had refused to attack Caesar's positions.</p><p>Scipio drew up his legions in three lines in the centre, with cavalry and light infantry on the flanks and the elephants in front of the flanks. Caesar also formed three lines: Legio VII and X on the right, VIII and IX on the left, and XIII and XIV in the centre with a newly recruited legion on either side of them, mixing recruits and veterans being one of his trademarks. His slingers, archers and cavalry stood on the flanks, and Legio V, split in two, was held in reserve behind the flanks to counter the elephants. Caesar commanded the right. He had left two recently recruited legions to continue the siege of the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In February <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> arrived at Thapsus and besieged it, surrounding it with a double line of circumvallation. The Marsh of Moknine left two landward approaches. Caesar fortified the southern one with three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, forcing his opponents to attack it or march round the marsh to the east. Scipio chose the second and placed Afranius and a few soldiers opposite the fortifications and Juba and Labienus with the Numidian cavalry south of the marsh. The main army marched to the eastern approach and began building a camp, covered by the rest of the army drawn up in battle formation.</p><p>Neither side committed to battle for some time. Then a trumpeter of the VII sounded the attack, and Caesar, seeing his right surge forward, ordered a general advance. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on the right attacked the elephants opposite them, which panicked and trampled their own men. On the other flank the elephants charged Caesar's left. His light infantry and cavalry moved out of their path, and five cohorts of Legio V Alaudae, placed behind the flank, sustained the charge, stabbing their pila at the elephants' eyes and weak spots and sounding their trumpets until the beasts ran back into their own right flank.</p><p>After the loss of the elephants Scipio began to lose ground, his left broke first and the rest followed. Caesar's cavalry outmanoeuvred its enemy and destroyed the fortified camp. The garrison of Thapsus sallied out against the siege works but was forced back by the two legions Caesar had left there, which marched south and overran Afranius's camp. Juba's allied troops abandoned the site before they could attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> went on to the Optimates' camp and found it already stormed. There he lost control of his own men, who began slaughtering their opponents. Around ten thousand enemies were killed, those who survived the battle being put to the sword by soldiers who ignored Caesar's repeated orders to spare them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports that according to some sources Caesar had an epileptic seizure just before he ordered his lines forward, causing confusion and disobeyed orders.</p><p>Scipio, Labienus, Juba, Afranius and Petreius escaped from the field. Labienus, with Sextus Pompeius and Varus, fled to Gnaeus Pompeius, who was raising forces on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Afranius and Faustus Cornelius Sulla collected survivors and began to pillage eastern Mauretania, whose king had sided with Caesar, but were caught by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sittius">Publius Sittius</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> commander working for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii">Bocchus II</a> and an ally of Caesar, and executed a few days later. Juba and Petreius fled to Numidia, and with Sittius closing in they chose to die by duelling each other. According to De Bello Africo, Juba killed Petreius and then had a slave kill him.</p><p>Caesar renewed the siege of Thapsus, which eventually fell, and went on to Utica, where Cato was garrisoned. On news of the defeat Cato killed himself. Plutarch reports that Caesar said, "Cato, I must grudge you your death, as you grudged me the honour of saving your life." Scipio tried to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> with a small fleet, but bad weather drove him back to the African coast, where Sittius and his fleet caught him off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>. After losing the naval engagement, Scipio stabbed himself with his sword.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known in part from De Bello Africo, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> work continuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s accounts of his campaigns against his Republican enemies in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It is the source for the deaths of Juba and Petreius in their duel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> supplies two further reports: that according to some sources Caesar had an epileptic seizure just before he ordered his lines forward, causing confusion and disobeyed orders, and the remark Caesar made on hearing of Cato's death.</p><p>Estimates of both armies' strengths are round ranges, 60,000 to 70,000 for Caesar and around 90,000 for the Optimates, and the ten thousand enemy dead is a single round figure. The seizure is reported only as the view of some sources, and it is one explanation offered for the slaughter that Caesar could not stop.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompey.jpg" alt="Pompey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Pompey_the_Great,_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle brought peace to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> left and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 25 July of the same year. The Optimate cause was not finished, since Labienus, the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, Varus and several others gathered another army in Baetica in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a>. The civil war was not over, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-munda">Battle of Munda</a> was to follow.</p><p>The deaths of Scipio and Cato, of Juba and Petreius, and of Afranius and Faustus Cornelius Sulla removed most of the leadership that had held the African provinces against Caesar. Caesar is reported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> to have been upset at Cato's suicide.</p><p>The Battle of Thapsus is generally regarded as marking the last large-scale use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> in the West. Legio V Alaudae was awarded an elephant as its symbol for its stand against them.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Thapsus on 6 April 46 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a>'s twelve legions routed the Optimate army of Scipio and King Juba of Numidia, said to number around 90,000, after the elephants panicked. Scipio, Cato, Juba and Petreius killed themselves soon after, but the civil war went on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>.</em></p>

